-- rules/ingest.rofl — INDEXING A CORPUS, AS A PROGRAM THAT DRIVES ITSELF.
--
-- The caller states a GOAL and nothing else:
--
--   must_index[code](Corpus, File).   one per file a named corpus must hold
--
-- and this file derives the three things a driver needs: whether the goal is
-- met, WHICH files are missing, and what to do next. Nothing here parses
-- anything or touches a disk; those are the host's, and they reach the world
-- only as facts it asserts back.
--
-- WHY THIS IS RULES AND NOT A LOOP IN TYPESCRIPT. A driver written as code
-- knows what it did; a driver written as rules can be ASKED. `needs_index` is
-- a query, not a variable — so "is the corpus indexed" and "what is left" are
-- the same kind of question as anything else in this system, answerable after
-- a crash, from a snapshot, by someone who did not write the loop. And when
-- the answer is wrong, the disagreement is with a rule that can be read.
--
-- THE TICK IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE, and it is not a detail. `Assumption` freezes
-- what `not p` is judged against for a whole ROUND (docs/volumes-and-residency
-- .md), so `needs_index` is computed against a world that does not move while
-- the host works through it. A file parsed mid-round does not silently vanish
-- from the frontier the host is holding. It vanishes at the BOUNDARY, which is
-- also the only place a volume may be lifted or dropped — and that is the same
-- sentence said twice, because loading and cooling are the same operation with
-- opposite sign.

-- ZERO-ARITY IS NOT IN THIS LANGUAGE, and the goal is better for it. `complete.`
-- does not parse — `args` requires at least one term, and both engines refuse
-- it identically — so the status relations carry the CORPUS they are about.
-- Which is what you want anyway: one world can hold several indexing goals,
-- and that is exactly the shape a corpus split into volumes takes.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- indexed

-- A file is indexed when its AST root is in the world. `ast_file` is
-- scanners/js_ast.ts's one-per-parsed-file fact, so this asks the data rather
-- than a bookkeeping flag the host would have to remember to set.
indexed[code](F) :- ast_file[code](_, F).

-- ...OR WHEN ITS FACTS HAVE BEEN COOLED TO DISK. This is the load-bearing
-- clause of the whole file. The facts are gone; the knowledge that they
-- existed, and where they went, is not — otherwise cooling would put the file
-- straight back on the frontier and the loop would index it forever.
--
-- `cooled[code](File, Path)` is written by the host at the moment it finishes
-- writing the book out, never before: a crash between the write and the fact
-- costs a re-parse, which is the cheap direction. The other order would claim
-- a file is on disk when it is not.
indexed[code](F) :- cooled[code](F, _).

-- --------------------------------------------------------------- the goal

-- THE FRONTIER, and the list the caller asked for.
needs_index[code](C, F) :- must_index[code](C, F), not indexed[code](F).

-- WHETHER THE GOAL IS MET. Two relations rather than one negation at the call
-- site, because `complete` has to be a FACT a driver can stop on, and `not
-- needs_index(_)` is not a fact — it is a question whose answer depends on
-- which round asked it.
index_incomplete[code](C) :- needs_index[code](C, _).
index_complete[code](C)   :- must_index[code](C, _),
                             not index_incomplete[code](C).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- the intent

-- Identity is the tuple `(Kind, Inquiry, Target)` and there are no fresh ids
-- in logic — the same discipline rules/inquiry/intents.rofl states, for the
-- same reason: an id minted in a rule is an id that changes when the rule
-- fires twice, and then nothing downstream can be compared across ticks.
candidate_intent(parse, C, F) :- needs_index[code](C, F).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- cooling

-- A file's book may be cooled once it is indexed and nothing still hot needs
-- it. `needed_hot` is EMPTY for a pure AST index, and that is exactly why 128
-- volumes came out self-sufficient — the JS model does not resolve across
-- module boundaries yet. When it does, this is the relation that stops the
-- loop from cooling a book somebody is still reading, and it is stated here
-- now so that the day it fills, nothing else has to change.
coolable[code](F) :- indexed[code](F), not needed_hot[code](F).

-- WHEN TO COOL IS A DISJUNCTION, AND ONE SIGNAL WAS NOT ENOUGH.
--
-- This began as a single premise on `peak_rows` against `space`, and that is
-- the WRONG INSTRUMENT for the workload it was written for. Measured on the
-- whole of eslint, 1426 files: the world grew to 5 896 383 facts while
-- `peak_rows` sat at 7 437 against a 25 000 threshold, so cooling never fired
-- once. The join accumulator is empty on a pure AST index, because every fact
-- is BASE and nothing is derived from it — the store grows and the accumulator
-- does not. Rows are not facts, and a threshold on one is blind to the other.
--
-- So the reasons are separate facts and any of them is enough. Each is
-- measured by a DIFFERENT instrument and none can stand in for another, which
-- is the whole lesson: a single number that correlates with pressure is not
-- pressure. The host asserts whichever hold; the rules still decide WHAT may
-- be cooled and never why.
under_pressure[code](C) :- rows_pressure[code](C).
under_pressure[code](C) :- world_pressure[code](C).
under_pressure[code](C) :- memory_pressure[code](C).
under_pressure[code](C) :- volume_settled[code](C).
under_pressure[code](C) :- eviction_asked[code](C).

edb(rows_pressure).      -- the join accumulator nears `space`
edb(world_pressure).     -- the STORE nears its fact ceiling; the one that was missing
edb(memory_pressure).    -- resident memory, which only the host can see at all
edb(volume_settled).     -- the work is done and nothing pending refers to it
edb(eviction_asked).     -- a checkpoint, a shutdown, a snapshot: somebody said so
--
-- The rules decide WHAT to cool; the host decides WHETHER it must. Neither
-- half can do the other's job: the host cannot see which book is still wanted,
-- and the rules cannot see how much room is left.
-- PREMISE ORDER IS THE AUTHOR'S AND IT MATTERS HERE. `must_index` leads
-- because it binds BOTH C and F; leading with `coolable(F)` and
-- `under_pressure(C)` pairs two relations that share no variable, which is a
-- cross product — `test/rule-shape.test.ts` counts those against a baseline
-- and caught this one the first time it ran.
candidate_intent(cool, C, F) :- must_index[code](C, F),
                                under_pressure[code](C),
                                coolable[code](F),
                                not cooled[code](F, _).

-- WHAT A COLD BOOK OWES A QUESTION, and it is a refusal rather than silence.
--
-- After cooling, the file's facts are not in the world. A question about them
-- must therefore REFUSE — `hole` is this kernel's word for "this is not an
-- answer" — because an empty answer and a clean one are the same two
-- characters, and a corpus that cooled half of itself would otherwise report
-- half of everything as absent rather than as unloaded.
--
-- THE HOST WRITES THE HOLE, not this file: `hole` is a kernel relation and a
-- rule may not conclude into one. That is the right division anyway. Cooling
-- IS a host act — it writes bytes to a disk — and the kernel records what the
-- host says it did, which is the same shape as every other assertion trail
-- here. The rule below is what a reader should look for when a question about
-- a cold file comes back empty instead of refusing.
--
--   the host asserts, at the moment the book leaves memory:
--     cooled[code](File, Path).
--     hole($cold(File), cooled_to_disk).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------ the census

-- The two numbers a driver prints, derived rather than counted in the host:
-- what was asked for, and what is left. A count is not expressible here, so
-- these are the SETS and the host counts them — which keeps the arithmetic
-- outside the rules and the membership inside, where it can be argued with.
index_target[code](C, F) :- must_index[code](C, F).
index_left[code](C, F)   :- needs_index[code](C, F).
index_done[code](C, F)   :- must_index[code](C, F), indexed[code](F).
index_cold[code](C, F)   :- must_index[code](C, F), cooled[code](F, _).
